text":"a verb describing movement"},{"id":"D","text":"a name beginning with Lake"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Identify the geographical category before applying its naming convention.","explanation":"The name **Lake Geneva** is a name beginning with Lake; that category helps explain its conventional article use.","keywords":["name-category"],"skill":"name-category","contextKey":"articles-place-names-50","fingerprint":"cefc1943cf7417ce0601b21545fcdb0cb24619e4af0cc6d820b188ce1a6e5da8","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-zero-articles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Identify the geographical category before applying its naming convention.","The name **Lake Geneva** is a name beginning with Lake; that category helps explain its conventional article use.","Choose **a name beginning with Lake**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This does not describe the geographical name Lake Geneva; it is a name beginning with Lake.","B":"This does not describe the geographical name Lake Geneva; it is a name beginning with Lake.","C":"This does not describe the geographical name Lake Geneva; it is a name beginning with Lake.","D":"The name **Lake Geneva** is a name beginning with Lake; that category helps explain its conventional article use."}}},{"id":"5a5246cc-f175-5228-a187-90ef285f9536","topicSlug":"articles-place-names","level":"A2","prompt":"Choose the ordinary geographical name phrase to complete “Our lesson concerned ____.” The place is lake geneva (a name beginning with Lake).","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"a Lake Geneva"},{"id":"B","text":"an Lake Geneva"},{"id":"C","text":"the Lake Geneva"},{"id":"D","text":"Lake Geneva"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Build the complete noun phrase from the place category and its established name.","explanation":"The complete ordinary name phrase is **Lake Geneva**. Keep the established article convention and the proper-name capitals.","keywords":["complete-name-phrase"],"skill":"complete-name-phrase","contextKey":"articles-place-names-50","fingerprint":"73746ae4b17ecd0449508ea2cbc9e97cf9b1c70762c30b5ee4b3ad4c02255c2e","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-zero-articles"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Build the complete noun phrase from the place category and its established name.","The complete ordinary name phrase is **Lake Geneva**. Keep the established article convention and the proper-name capitals.","Choose **Lake Geneva**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A does not give the ordinary geographical naming pattern requested.","B":"An does not give the ordinary geographical naming pattern requested.","C":"The ordinary naming convention here requires **Lake Geneva**.","D":"The complete ordinary name phrase is **Lake Geneva**. Keep the established article convention and the proper-name capitals."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Learn common geographical patterns and distinguish an institution’s purpose from its physical building.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Names that usually take no article","explanation":"Most ordinary country and city names take no article, as do continents, individual mountains named with Mount, and lakes named with Lake. These are naming conventions rather than a universal rule for all proper nouns.","pattern":"**France**, **Tokyo**, **Mount Fuji**, **Lake Victoria**","commonMistakes":["The Hague is a city-name exception. Check unfamiliar names rather than guessing solely from the place category."],"examples":["Use ordinary geographical reference to a country with an ordinary short name: “We studied a map of **—** France.” (— = no article)","Use ordinary geographical reference to a country with an ordinary short name: “We studied a map of **—** Japan.” (— = no article)"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Names that commonly take the","explanation":"River, sea, ocean and mountain-range names commonly take **the**. Some country names also do, especially plural names or names containing words such as Kingdom and Republic. Short and full official names can behave differently.","pattern":"**the Nile**, **the Alps**, **the United Kingdom**","commonMistakes":["Czechia normally has no article; the Czech Republic takes the. The chosen name matters."],"examples":["Complete ordinary naming inside an of-phrase: “The geography of **—** France was our subject.”","Complete ordinary naming inside an of-phrase: “The geography of **—** Japan was our subject.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Institutional purpose and the building","explanation":"In British English, go to school, be in prison and be in hospital can refer to the institution’s usual role. The school or the hospital often identifies a particular building or site. American usage differs notably for in the hospital.","pattern":"The pupil is **at school**. / The builder is **at the school**.","commonMistakes":["Do not mark American in the hospital wrong without specifying the variety and intended meaning."],"examples":["Which category explains the ordinary article pattern for “France”? → **a country with an ordinary short name**","Which category explains the ordinary article pattern for “Japan”? → **a country with an ordinary short name**"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Names inside larger noun phrases","explanation":"A geographical name can occur inside a larger noun phrase, where an article may belong to another noun. The history of France has the before history, not before France. A new context can also make a proper name descriptive: the Paris of my childhood.","pattern":"**The history of France** is the subject.","commonMistakes":["The exercises test ordinary geographical reference, not special descriptive or metaphorical uses of names."],"examples":["Choose the ordinary geographical name phrase to complete “Our lesson concerned **France**.” The place is france (a country with an ordinary short name).","Choose the ordinary geographical name phrase to complete “Our lesson concerned **Japan**.” The place is japan (a country with an ordinary short name)."],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**France**, **Tokyo**, **Mount Fuji**, **Lake Victoria**","**the Nile**, **the Alps**, **the United Kingdom**","The pupil is **at school**. / The builder is **at the school**.","**The history of France** is the subject."]},"stage":"2 · Everyday descriptions","prerequisites":["movement-prepositions"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
